<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Hellenic State (1941-1944)</h1><p class="st-meta">1941 CE – 1944 CE · capital Athens</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greece's collaborationist government during the Axis occupation, which ruled in Athens under German and Italian control during famine and resistance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in April 1941, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-tsolakoglou">Georgios Tsolakoglou</a> surrendered the army and was installed as prime minister of a collaborationist government in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-of-greece">King George II</a> and the legitimate government fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The country was divided into German, Italian and Bulgarian occupation zones.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime had little real authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> requisitions and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> caused the great famine of 1941-1942, in which tens of thousands died in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-tsolakoglou">Tsolakoglou</a> was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantinos-logothetopoulos">Konstantinos Logothetopoulos</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-rallis">Ioannis Rallis</a>, who formed the Security Battalions to fight the communist-led resistance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-greece">EAM-ELAS</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> were deported in 1943 with little resistance from the government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As German forces withdrew in October 1944, the government collapsed and the government-in-exile returned. Its leaders were tried as collaborators in 1945. The bitter divisions of the occupation fed directly into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-civil-war">Greek Civil War</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1941 CE – 1944 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:98.33%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1941 CE – 1944 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Athens</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Georgios Tsolakoglou, Konstantinos Logothetopoulos, Ioannis Rallis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Greek collaborationist government, Elliniki Politeia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=hellenic-state-1941-1944" aria-label="Hellenic State (1941-1944) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5271 812 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hellenic State (1941-1944) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5591 1068L5300 622M5591 1068L6173 1949M5591 1068L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 622h0M6173 1949h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Athens. <a href="/atlas?year=1942&amp;focus=hellenic-state-1941-1944">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
